    <h1>Block Blast Hints: 10+ Strategies &amp; Tips for a Higher Score</h1>

    <p>Block Blast looks deceptively simple — drag colored blocks onto an 8x8 board, clear full lines, repeat. The moment your board fills with awkward staircases and you are counting the pixels left in the final column, you realize the game is a genuine spatial-planning challenge. These Block Blast hints are organized by skill tier, so you can read the whole set or jump straight to the level that matches where your games keep ending. Ten-plus strategies, five common mistakes to kill, and a high-score strategy section at the end.</p>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Hungry Studio.</div>

    <h2>Beginner Block Blast Hints: Build a Survival Board</h2>
    <p>Your first goal is not a high score — it is a longer game. Every hint in this tier is about living past round 5 consistently.</p>

    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-beginner">Beginner Hint 1</span>
      <strong>Keep the board flat. Flat boards survive; towers die.</strong>
      <p>Tall single-column stacks are the #1 killer of beginner games. The moment one column rises above its neighbors, you need a 1xN block shaped exactly like the gap — and the game frequently refuses to give you one. Spread blocks evenly and the board stays forgiving.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-beginner">Beginner Hint 2</span>
      <strong>Clear lines as soon as they complete.</strong>
      <p>Holding a completed line for a "perfect moment" is a beginner trap. Completed lines tie up cells and make later placements harder. Clear immediately, keep the open space, and let survival do the scoring.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-beginner">Beginner Hint 3</span>
      <strong>Always keep a buffer strip of empty cells.</strong>
      <p>Try to keep at least one full empty row or column available at all times. It acts as a parking lot for awkward pieces and as an emergency line-clear when the board gets tight. If every cell is spoken for, you are one bad piece away from game over.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-beginner">Beginner Hint 4</span>
      <strong>Watch the three-piece queue before every move.</strong>
      <p>You can see your next three blocks at all times. Before placing anything, check the queue and ask: "Do any of these fill a line if I set up now?" Preparing for the pieces you can see is the difference between reacting and playing.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Intermediate Block Blast Hints: Plan Combos</h2>
    <p>Once you survive consistently, the scoreboard becomes the game. Combo lines are where the points are, and these hints are all about creating them on purpose.</p>

    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-intermediate">Intermediate Hint 5</span>
      <strong>Build toward double and triple line clears.</strong>
      <p>Clearing two lines at once roughly doubles your points for those lines, and three-line clears are worth even more per block. Instead of clearing the first complete line, see whether one more piece can complete two lines simultaneously. The "one more piece" mindset is the biggest score jump in the game.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-intermediate">Intermediate Hint 6</span>
      <strong>Use the "domino gap" technique.</strong>
      <p>When a row is one cell short, leave the gap — and keep a column beside it one cell shallow. A single 1x2 block can then complete both the row and the column in one move: a double clear from two cells. Setup like this turns accidental combos into planned ones.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-intermediate">Intermediate Hint 7</span>
      <strong>Plan two moves ahead, not one.</strong>
      <p>Before placing a piece, imagine the next piece in the queue landing too. If your placement makes the next piece unplaceable, adjust now. Two-move planning eliminates most mid-game disasters before they happen.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-intermediate">Intermediate Hint 8</span>
      <strong>Reserve the corners for exactly the right moment.</strong>
      <p>Corner cells are easy to fill but awkward to clear. Do not dump 2x2 squares into corners out of habit. Leave flexible corner space so a big square piece has a home — a 2x2 or 3x3 placed in a corner clears massive area when its lines complete.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Advanced Block Blast Hints: Play the Piece Economy</h2>
    <p>Advanced play is about understanding that every piece is a resource and every cell is a promise. These hints take you from "good" to "top of the leaderboard."</p>

    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-advanced">Advanced Hint 9</span>
      <strong>Sacrifice small pieces to fix gaps.</strong>
      <p>When a bad gap appears, resist the urge to hold your big piece for a big clear. Use a small piece to patch the gap so the board stays flat. A flat board generates many small clears; a gapped board generates a game over. Piece economy beats piece vanity.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-advanced">Advanced Hint 10</span>
      <strong>Keep an empty column all game long.</strong>
      <p>Elite players maintain a dedicated empty column as a reservoir. It absorbs stair-steps, gives 1xN pieces a home, and — when the board gets critical — becomes a completed line you can clear for breathing room. One permanent empty column is worth more than any single combo.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-advanced">Advanced Hint 11</span>
      <strong>Predict the worst case, not the average case.</strong>
      <p>The block sequence is random, so plan for the worst plausible next piece. If your board only survives a 1x4, but the game can deal a 2x2, you are gambling. Boards that survive their worst-case piece are boards that reach round 30+.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-advanced">Advanced Hint 12</span>
      <strong>Count your cells like a chess player counts material.</strong>
      <p>Sum the sizes of the three queued pieces and compare against your free space. If free space &lt; queued size, you must clear a line this turn or you lose. That simple arithmetic tells you exactly when to abandon combo plans and prioritize survival.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>5 Common Block Blast Mistakes (and How to Stop Making Them)</h2>

    <div class="miss-card">
      <span class="pill pill-error">Mistake 1</span>
      <strong>One tall tower in the corner.</strong>
      <p>It feels safe because it is "out of the way" — until the only piece that fits is a horizontal 1x5 and there is no room left on either side. Never let any column lead the board by more than 2 cells.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="miss-card">
      <span class="pill pill-error">Mistake 2</span>
      <strong>Clearing every line instantly, zero combo planning.</strong>
      <p>Instant clears are safe but low-scoring. Train yourself to check whether one more piece can complete a second line before you pull the trigger. It is a habit, and habits score.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="miss-card">
      <span class="pill pill-error">Mistake 3</span>
      <strong>Ignoring the queue and playing reactively.</strong>
      <p>If you place pieces without checking the next three, you are leaving points on the table and signing up for avoidable disasters. The queue is not a hint — it is the rules of the next three turns.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="miss-card">
      <span class="pill pill-error">Mistake 4</span>
      <strong>Cornering every 2x2 square.</strong>
      <p>The classic beginner habit. A 2x2 in a corner fills 4 cells that are painful to clear. Place squares centrally where they can complete two lines at once instead.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="miss-card">
      <span class="pill pill-error">Mistake 5</span>
      <strong>Refusing to sacrifice a piece.</strong>
      <p>"I will save this 1x5 for the perfect moment" is how games die. If the 1x5 does not fit anywhere without destroying your board structure, use a smaller piece to stabilize and move on. Board health outranks piece ego.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>How to Get a High Score in Block Blast: Strategy, Step by Step</h2>
    <div class="score-box">
      <p>The full high-score loop, in order:</p>
      <p><strong>1. Open with a flat spread.</strong> Place the first pieces spaced out, never stacked in one area.</p>
      <p><strong>2. Establish your permanent empty column.</strong> Pick the rightmost column and defend it all game.</p>
      <p><strong>3. Set up the domino gap.</strong> Keep one row one-cell-short with its neighboring column one-cell-shallow.</p>
      <p><strong>4. Clear in pairs.</strong> Complete rows in pairs or triples for combo multipliers, using the queue to time the final piece.</p>
      <p><strong>5. Rebalance after every clear.</strong> Each line clear creates new gaps — immediately smooth them with small pieces.</p>
      <p><strong>6. Count down to the endgame.</strong> When free space gets tight, switch from combo mode to survival mode: clear the fastest line available, every turn.</p>
    </div>
    <p>Follow that loop and the score improvement is not gradual — it is a step change. The first time you survive to round 20 with a flat board, you will wonder how you ever played any other way.</p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

