    <details><summary>How do you get a high score in Block Blast?</summary><p>High scores come from combos and survival, not from raw clearing. Clear two or more lines at once to trigger combo multipliers, and stay alive long enough to reach the later rounds where every line is worth more. The domino strategy — leaving a one-cell gap next to a near-full line so a 1x2 block can complete it — is one of the most reliable combo builders.</p></details>

    <details><summary>How many blocks can you hold in Block Blast?</summary><p>Block Blast shows you three upcoming pieces at a time. You can drag any of the three onto the board in any order. A placed piece locks in place immediately — it cannot be moved, rotated, or undone — so plan the whole placement before you drag. Use that certainty: count the three queued pieces and prepare the exact gap each one will fill.</p></details>

    <details><summary>What are the common mistakes beginners make in Block Blast?</summary><p>The five most common beginner mistakes are: stacking in one tall column, clearing lines immediately without planning combos, ignoring the block queue, placing 2x2 squares in the corners, and refusing to sacrifice a small piece to fix a bad gap. Fix those five and your average game will easily double in length.</p></details>

